              "abstractOriginal": "Machine learning interatomic potentials (MLIPs) are essential components for accelerating simulation-driven materials design. Data-efficient MLIP training relies on data-selection strategies that maximize structural diversity while limiting computationally expensive first-principles calculations. A key challenge in such strategies is evaluating structural similarity, which involves a trade-off between retaining information on individual atomic environments and reducing computational cost. Here, we propose similarity evaluation methods that achieve both representational fidelity and computational efficiency. Our method represents each structure using a small set of characteristic atomic environments identified by k-medoids clustering and computes pairwise similarity through optimal matching between these representatives or their distributions. Molecular benchmarks demonstrate that our method is approximately 50 times faster than the baseline method while also more clearly distinguishing structures with different chemical compositions. Similarity-based data-selection benchmarks demonstrate that our methods improve the data efficiency and stability of force prediction in MLIPs.",
              "abstractZh": "机器学习原子间势 (MLIP) 是加速模拟驱动的材料设计的重要组成部分。数据高效的 MLIP 训练依赖于数据选择策略，该策略可最大限度地提高结构多样性，同时限制计算成本高昂的第一性原理计算。这种策略的一个关键挑战是评估结构相似性，这涉及保留单个原子环境的信息和降低计算成本之间的权衡。 在这里，我们提出了同时实现表征保真度和计算效率的相似性评估方法。我们的方法使用由 k-medoids 聚类识别的一小组特征原子环境来表示每个结构，并通过这些代表或其分布之间的最佳匹配来计算成对相似性。分子基准测试表明，我们的方法比基线方法快约 50 倍，同时还可以更清晰地区分具有不同化学成分的结构。 基于相似性的数据选择基准表明，我们的方法提高了 MLIP 中力预测的数据效率和稳定性。",
              "articleSummary": "Machine learning interatomic potentials (MLIPs) are essential components for accelerating simulation-driven materials design. Data-efficient MLIP training relies on data-selection strategies that maximize structural diversity while limiting computationally expensive first-principles calculations. A key challenge in such strategies is evaluating structural similarity, which involves a trade-off between retaining information on individual atomic environments and reducing computational cost. Here, we propose similarity evaluation methods that achieve both representational fidelity and computational efficiency. Our method represents each structure using a small set of characteristic atomic environments identified by k-medoids clustering and computes pairwise similarity through optimal matching between these representatives or their distributions. Molecular benchmarks demonstrate that our method is approximately 50 times faster than the baseline method while also more clearly distinguishing structures with different chemical compositions. Similarity-based data-selection benchmarks demonstrate that our methods improve the data efficiency and stability of force prediction in MLIPs.",
              "articleSummaryZh": "机器学习原子间势 (MLIP) 是加速模拟驱动的材料设计的重要组成部分。数据高效的 MLIP 训练依赖于数据选择策略，该策略可最大限度地提高结构多样性，同时限制计算成本高昂的第一性原理计算。这种策略的一个关键挑战是评估结构相似性，这涉及保留单个原子环境的信息和降低计算成本之间的权衡。 在这里，我们提出了同时实现表征保真度和计算效率的相似性评估方法。我们的方法使用由 k-medoids 聚类识别的一小组特征原子环境来表示每个结构，并通过这些代表或其分布之间的最佳匹配来计算成对相似性。分子基准测试表明，我们的方法比基线方法快约 50 倍，同时还可以更清晰地区分具有不同化学成分的结构。 基于相似性的数据选择基准表明，我们的方法提高了 MLIP 中力预测的数据效率和稳定性。",
